.TH DD 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
dd \- convert and copy a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dd
.RI [ operand = value ] .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dd
copies the input named by
.B if=
(or standard input, if
.B if=
is not given) to the output named by
.B of=
(or standard output), reading and writing it in blocks.
Unlike most utilities,
.BR dd 's
own arguments are not
.B \-flag
options: every argument must be an
.IR operand = value
pair, and any argument that is not (or whose operand name is not one of
those listed below) is a diagnosed error.
.PP
At the end of copying, whether it finished normally or was interrupted
(see
.B EXIT STATUS
below),
.B dd
writes a summary line pair to standard error in the form
.RS
.nf
N+P records in
N+P records out
.fi
.RE
giving the number of full and partial blocks read and, separately, the
number of full and partial blocks written.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.BI if= file
Read input from
.I file
instead of standard input.
.TP
.BI of= file
Write output to
.I file
instead of standard output.
.I file
is truncated to zero length before writing begins, unless
.B conv=notrunc
is also given (see below).
.TP
.BI ibs= expr
Set the input block size (default 512 bytes). See
.B BLOCK SIZE EXPRESSIONS
below.
.TP
.BI obs= expr
Set the output block size (default 512 bytes).
.TP
.BI bs= expr
Set both the input and the output block size to
.IR expr ,
and switch to a different copying strategy: each block actually read is
written out immediately at whatever length was read, rather than being
repacked into
.BR obs=-sized
output blocks the way
.B ibs=
/
.B obs=
copying does. Whichever of
.BR bs= ,
.BR ibs= ,
and
.B obs=
is given last on the command line for a given size wins, in ordinary
left-to-right order, but giving
.B bs=
at all selects the direct-write strategy just described regardless of
where it appears.
.TP
.BI count= n
Copy only
.I n
input blocks, then stop; a short final block still counts as one block
toward
.IR n .
.TP
.BI skip= n
Skip
.I n
.BR ibs= -sized
blocks at the start of the input before copying begins. If the input
is not seekable, the skipped bytes are read and discarded instead;
skipping past the actual end of the input is not an error.
.TP
.BI seek= n
Skip
.I n
.BR obs= -sized
blocks at the start of the output before copying begins (used to
overwrite part of an existing file without disturbing the rest).
Unlike
.BR skip= ,
if the output is not seekable this is a diagnosed error rather than
falling back to any other behavior.
.TP
.BI conv= value [, value \fR.\|.\|.\fP]
A comma-separated list of conversions to apply while copying. Only the
following values are implemented; any other value is refused with a
diagnostic and a nonzero exit status, never silently ignored.
.RS
.TP
.B notrunc
Do not truncate the output file before writing (see
.B of=
above): bytes in it beyond what this run writes are left intact.
.TP
.B sync
Pad every short input block \(em whether from a final partial read, or
(combined with
.BR noerror )
one cut short by a read error \(em out to the full input block size
with trailing NUL bytes, instead of passing its actual short length
through unchanged.
.TP
.B noerror
Continue copying after a read error instead of stopping. The block
that failed contributes no data of its own (or, combined with
.BR sync ,
a full block of NUL bytes).
.RE
.SH BLOCK SIZE EXPRESSIONS
An
.I expr
given to
.BR ibs= ,
.BR obs= ,
or
.B bs=
is an unsigned decimal integer, optionally followed by one of the
suffixes
.B b
(multiply by 512),
.B k
(multiply by 1024), or
.B w
(multiply by the size in bytes of a C
.B int
on this platform, which is 4), and optionally followed by
.B x
and another such expression, meaning multiplication \(em so
.B 2x3x4
parses left to right as
.RI ( 2 * 3 )* 4.
A block size of zero, or an expression that does not parse in full, is
a diagnosed error.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
The
.B conv=
values
.BR ucase ,
.BR lcase ,
.BR swab ,
.BR ascii ,
.BR ebcdic ,
.BR block ,
and
.B unblock
found in other implementations of
.B dd
are not implemented here; giving any of them is refused as an
unrecognized
.B conv=
value rather than being silently ignored or partially applied.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The copy completed with no error.
.TP
.B 1
A read or write error occurred (and, for a read error,
.B conv=noerror
was not given), or a named
.B if=
or
.B of=
file could not be opened.
.TP
.B 2
A malformed
.IR operand = value
argument, an unrecognized operand name, or an invalid
.BR ibs= / obs= / bs= / count= / skip= / seek= / conv=
value.
.TP
.B 130
.B dd
was interrupted by SIGINT before it finished copying; the summary
described above is still written first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR cksum (1),
.BR tee (1)
